ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI: 10.1126/science.1250944 ... Funders: National Institutes of Health, Office of Naval Research, National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development\n# Polyhedra Self-Assembled from DNA Tripods and Characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT\n\nOpenAlex ID: W2100830203\n\nDOI: 10.1126/science.1250944\n\nPMID: 24625926\n\nType: article\n\nYear: 2014\n\nPublication date: 2014-03-14\n\nLanguage: en\n\n## Source\n\nName: Science\n\nISSN-L: 0036-8075\n\nISSN: 0036-8075, 1095-9203\n\nPublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\n\n## Citation\n\nVolume: 344\n\nIssue: 6179\n\nPages: 65–69\n\n## Article links\n\n  * Article website\n  * Location website\n  * Location website\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Ryosuke Iinuma — Harvard University (ROR)\n  * Yonggang Ke (ORCID) — Harvard University (ROR); Dana-Farber Cancer Institute (ROR)\n  * Ralf Jungmann (ORCID) — Harvard University (ROR); Center for Systems Biology (ROR)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2015 Issue\n2014\n \nサ\nイ\nエ\nン\nス\n誌\nに\n載\nっ\nた\n日\n本\n人\n研\n究\n者 (https://www.asca-co.com/ascalibrary/pdf_japanese_scientists/Science_2014.pdf)\nciteturn90search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; DOI: 10.1126/science.1250944\n4月4日号Research Article\nScience 4 April 2014: Vol. 344  no. 6179  pp. 65-69\nDOI: 10.1126/science.1250944\n4月4日号Research Article\nDNA Tripodを用いて自己組織化された多面体と \n3次元DNA-PAINT法による観測\nPolyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT\n飯沼 良介 Ryosuke Iinuma\nWyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University\n（現 JSRライフサイエンス株式会社 研究開発部）\nYonggang Ke\n1,2,3*   Ralf Jungmann\n1,4   Thomas Schlichthaerle\n1 \nJohannes B. Woehrstein\n1,4   Peng Yin\n1,4\n左から飯沼 良介、Ralf Jungmann、Yonggang Ke\nContact\n1 Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University\n2 Department of Cancer Biology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Harvard Medical \nSchool\n3 Department of Biological Chemistry and Molecular Pharmacology, Harvard \nMedical School\n4 Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School\n*現 Assistant professor of Wallace H. Coulter Department of Biomedical \nEngineering, Georgia Institute of Technology and Emory University\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications - Professur für AI-Guided Protein Design (https://www.bio.nat.tum.de/aipd/publications/)\nciteturn90search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; (Open Access) DOI: 10.1126/science.1250944\n\n## Publications\n\nList of all publications can be found on Google Scholar.\n\n### Most important publications\n\n  1. R. Iinuma*, Y. Ke*, R. Jungmann*, T. Schlichthaerle, J. B. Woehrstein, P. Yin, Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT. Science. 344, 65–69 (2014). (Open Access) DOI: 10.1126/science.1250944\n\n  2. T. Schlichthaerle, A. S. Eklund, F. Schueder, M. T. Strauss, C. Tiede, A. Curd, J. Ries, M. Peckham, D. C. Tomlinson, R. Jungmann, Site-Specific Labeling of Affimers for DNA-PAINT Microscopy. Angew. Chem. Int. Ed Engl. 57, 11060–11063 (2018). (Open Access) DOI: 10.1002/anie.201804020\n\n  3. T. Schlichthaerle*, M. Ganji*, A. Auer, O. Kimbu Wade, R. Jungmann, Bacterially Derived Antibody Binders as Small Adapters for DNA-PAINT Microscopy. Chembiochem. 20, 1032–1038 (2019). (Open Access) DOI: 10.1002/cbic.201800743\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT - Technical University of Munich (https://portal.fis.tum.de/en/publications/polyhedra-self-assembled-from-dna-tripods-and-characterized-with-/)\nciteturn90search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1250944 ... 2014;344(6179):65-69. doi: 10.1126/science.1250944\nWe experimentally constructed a tetrahedron (20 megadaltons), a triangular prism (30 megadaltons), a cube (40 megadaltons), a pentagonal prism (50 megadaltons), and a hexagonal prism (60 megadaltons) with edge widths of 100 nanometers. The structures were visualized by means of transmission electron microscopy and three-dimensional DNA-PAINT super-resolution fluorescent microscopy of single molecules in solution.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nPages (from-to)  | 65-69\nNumber of pages  | 5\nJournal  | Science\nVolume  | 344\nIssue number  | 6179\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1250944\nState  | Published - 2014\nExternally published  | Yes\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * 10.1126/science.1250944\n\n## Cite this\n\n  * APA\n  * Author\n  * BIBTEX\n  * Harvard\n  * Standard\n  * RIS\n  * Vancouver\n\nIinuma, R., Ke, Y., Jungmann, R., Schlichthaerle, T., Woehrstein, J. B., & Yin, P. (2014). Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT. Science, 344(6179), 65-69. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1250944\n\nIinuma, Ryosuke ; Ke, Yonggang ; Jungmann, Ralf et al. / Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT. In: Science. 2014 ; Vol. 344, No. 6179. pp. 65-69.\n\n@article{6a624967b6d0407baaac03080d3a455e,\n\ntitle = \"Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT\",\n\nabstract = \"DNA self-assembly has produced diverse synthetic three-dimensional polyhedra. These structures typically have a molecular weight no greater than 5 megadaltons. We report a simple, general strategy for one-step self-assembly of wireframe DNA polyhedra that are more massive than most previous structures. A stiff three-arm-junction DNA origami tile motif with precisely controlled angles and arm lengths was used for hierarchical assembly of polyhedra. We experimentally constructed a tetrahedron (20 megadaltons), a triangular prism (30 megadaltons), a cube (40 megadaltons), a pentagonal prism (50 megadaltons), and a hexagonal prism (60 megadaltons) with edge widths of 100 nanometers. The structures were visualized by means of transmission electron microscopy and three-dimensional DNA-PAINT super-resolution fluorescent microscopy of single molecules in solution.\",\n\nauthor = \"Ryosuke Iinuma and Yonggang Ke and Ralf Jungmann and Thomas Schlichthaerle and Woehrstein, \\{Johannes B.\\} and Peng Yin\",\n\nyear = \"2014\",\n\ndoi = \"10.1126/science.1250944\",\n\nlanguage = \"English\",\n\nvolume = \"344\",\n\npages = \"65--69\",\n\njournal = \"Science\",\n\nissn = \"0036-8075\",\n\npublisher = \"American Association for the Advancement of Science\",\n\nnumber = \"6179\",\n\n}\n\nIinuma, R, Ke, Y, Jungmann, R, Schlichthaerle, T, Woehrstein, JB & Yin, P 2014, 'Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT', Science, vol. 344, no. 6179, pp. 65-69. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1250944\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Polyhedra self-assembled from DNA tripods and characterized with 3D DNA-PAINT\n\nAU - Iinuma, Ryosuke\nThese structures typically have a molecular weight no greater than 5 megadaltons. We report a simple, general strategy for one-step self-assembly of wireframe DNA polyhedra that are more massive than most previous structures. A stiff three-arm-junction DNA origami tile motif with precisely controlled angles and arm lengths was used for hierarchical assembly of polyhedra. We experimentally constructed a tetrahedron (20 megadaltons), a triangular prism (30 megadaltons), a cube (40 megadaltons), a pentagonal prism (50 megadaltons), and a hexagonal prism (60 megadaltons) with edge widths of 100 nanometers. The structures were visualized by means of transmission electron microscopy and three-dimensional DNA-PAINT super-resolution fluorescent microscopy of single molecules in solution.\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84897583533\